# The Booth at the Perkins Off Highway 2

Gary Halstad is the first customer through the front door of the Perkins on a Friday morning at six-oh-eight.

He has been the first customer through the front door on a Friday morning since the second Friday after Marilyn — his wife Marilyn Ann Halstad, née Sundberg, forty-three years his wife, the second-grade teacher at Cohasset Elementary from the fall of 1969 through the spring of 2007, who kept every construction-paper valentine any child ever gave her in a plain cedar hope chest at the foot of the bed — was buried at Itasca Calvary Cemetery on a snowy Tuesday morning in the third week of January of 2015.

He is seventy-nine.

He orders, on Friday mornings, the Country Club Omelet.

He orders, on Tuesday mornings, the Grand Slam.

He has been ordering these two things in this order on these two mornings for eleven years and seven months, and Doreen Rekowski — who has waited on the same third booth from the door since the second week of April of 1997, who put her middle boy Cody through the LPN program at Itasca Community College on the tips from booths one through five of the north section, and who lost her own husband Ronnie on a July night on Highway 46 in 2011 — has not had to write down his order, or ask about it, or clarify it, since the second week of February of 2015.

She pours the second cup at six-nineteen without asking.

## What the ledger on Wisconsin Avenue does not have a field for

At nine-forty-four on the same Friday morning, ninety-one miles down the North Shore, in the second-floor office of the Second Harvest Northern Lakes Food Bank in a converted Great Northern Railway depot on West Superior Street in Duluth, a twenty-nine-year-old development associate named Hannah Elise Beaulieu — who came on in the September of 2024 out of the master's program in nonprofit administration at UWS — opens the third-quarter *lapsed and low-value monthly donors* tab of the food bank's Raiser's Edge dashboard.

Gary's line is on the fourth page, on the seventeenth row.

*HALSTAD, GARY R. — P.O. Box 741, Grand Rapids MN 55744. ACH $30/month since 8/2004. Lifetime giving $7,920. Non-metro. Non-alumnus. Wealth screen: NOT RUN (rural, deprioritized). No visits, no events, no engagement. Do not contact.*

The Raiser's Edge record does not have a field for the fact that on a Tuesday morning in the last week of the July of 2004 — the same summer Gary retired from thirty-nine years as a timber cruiser for Blandin Paper Company at their Grand Rapids mill, ten days after he had walked out of the North Wing offices with a small cardboard box containing a Weyerhaeuser tape measure, a Silva Ranger compass, and a plain steel Suunto clinometer his father had bought at the Sears & Roebuck in Duluth on a Saturday afternoon in the March of 1949 — Marilyn Halstad sat down at the small oak kitchen table in the little tan ranch on Fifth Avenue Northeast with a copy of the *Duluth News Tribune* open to a piece on the food shelf at Damiano in the West End, and said quietly, over a shared piece of coffee cake, that she thought they ought to send Second Harvest thirty dollars a month.

They did, and they have.

There is no field, either, for the fact that on a Wednesday morning in the fifth week of the June of 1971, a fifty-eight-year-old rangeland timberman named Reidar Osvald Halstad — Gary's father, Norwegian-born, arrived in Hibbing in the March of 1927 at seven, worked the Range from 1937 through the strike of 1949 and cruised timber for Boise Cascade from the spring of 1950 through the summer of 1970 — walked into the small storefront office of Karpa & Kittleson, attorneys at law, on East Fourth Street in Grand Rapids, and deeded to his son Gary, then twenty-four, a section of tax-forfeit land in Ardenhurst Township, four miles east of Marcell in Itasca County, that he had bought at the county auction in the November of 1963 for six hundred and forty-two dollars.

Six hundred and forty acres.

Not a fence, not a road, not a well, not a cabin.

Only, in the middle of a fen at the west edge of the section, in a stand nobody in Itasca County had cut since the Weyerhaeuser survey of 1902, three hundred and eleven acres of virgin white pine.

Reidar had never told anyone he owned it.

He told Gary once, in the front seat of a 1968 Chevrolet C10 pickup at eleven-fifty on a Wednesday morning in the second week of June, on the shoulder of County Road 45 half a mile short of the north property line, over a Thermos of coffee and a bologna sandwich on Bunny bread in wax paper, that he would like Gary to keep it standing.

Gary said he would.

He has kept it standing for fifty-five years.

## What the tape measure in the box under the bed says

In a small cardboard file box under the bed in the little tan ranch on Fifth Avenue Northeast, wedged between a plain white manila envelope of Marilyn's second-grade class photographs from 1974 through 2006 and a bundle of thirty-nine annual W-2s from Blandin Paper Company held together with a beige rubber band, is a plain manila folder marked, in Marilyn's blue Bic in the fall of 1994, *the section.*

Inside the folder is:

The recorded warranty deed from the county recorder's office in Grand Rapids, dated June 23, 1971, signed by Reidar O. Halstad and witnessed by an attorney named Sivert Wilhelm Karpa, who died in the November of 1991. Twenty-one hand-drawn timber cruise sheets in the plain narrow columns of a Wisconsin cruiser's field book, ranging from the September of 1971 through the September of 2023, in Gary's own hand in a pencil-perfect print he learned from a forestry short course at UMD in the March of 1968. A folded topographic map of the Chippewa National Forest quadrangle, marked in blue pencil along the west property line at the fen where the old-growth stand begins. A plain typewritten letter from a forestry consultant in Bemidji named Odell Marius Anderson, dated the fourth week of the June of 2024, giving a conservative fair-market appraisal for the standing timber alone at four million one hundred and twenty thousand dollars. A single sheet of yellow legal-pad paper, dated the last Thursday of the March of 2025, on which Gary — three months after he turned seventy-eight, sixteen days after the eleventh anniversary of Marilyn's burial — has written, in a print a little less pencil-perfect than the September of 1971 but still perfectly clear, seven quiet lines:

*Not to be cut. Not to be sold to be cut. Marilyn wanted the food shelves fed. The county has enough school-forest land already. Second Harvest, Duluth. In Marilyn's name. Ask Odell about the easement first.*

He has not, in the sixteen months since he wrote it, told a single person outside the two-story brick offices of Karpa & Kittleson on East Fourth Street.

He has not, in the sixteen months since he wrote it, mentioned it in a phone call, an email, an online form, a wealth-screening database, an alumni magazine, a class reunion, a fundraising event, a gala, an auction, or an *Ask Amy* column.

He has kept it, the way his father kept the section, in a plain manila folder in a plain cardboard box under the bed.

## The wrong question the industry has been asking for twenty-five years

For twenty-five years, the category of software the Second Harvest Northern Lakes Food Bank in Duluth has been sold — at increasingly expensive annual contracts, on second-quarter renewal calls in overheated conference rooms — has been asked, and has answered, one question:

*Of our metro alumni-adjacent donors, whose wealth-screen band changed this quarter?*

Gary is not an alumnus.

Gary lives ninety-one miles up Highway 2 from the office.

Gary has been on the *do-not-contact* tab, in one form or another, since a warm Tuesday morning in the last week of the July of 2004.

The tool has been asked the wrong question.

The question the tool would have needed to answer, on a Tuesday morning in the last week of the July of 2004, or on a Wednesday morning in the fifth week of the June of 1971, or on a Friday morning at six-nineteen in the second week of the August of 2026 — any of the three would have done — is smaller and harder and almost never appears on a keynote slide at a sector conference in a hotel across from an airport:

*Of the small non-metro ACHs on our monthly file, is there a widowed timber cruiser in Grand Rapids whose father put a section of virgin pine into his name on the twenty-third of June of 1971, and who has been carrying it alone since the third week of January of 2015, and who wrote seven lines on a yellow legal-pad in the March of 2025 that he has not yet told a soul about?*

That is not a wealth screen.

That is not a portfolio dashboard.

That is not a predictive-affinity model.

That is a small careful set of notes, assembled by somebody who has been sitting quietly with the small papers — with the plain half-column obituary for Marilyn Ann Halstad of Grand Rapids in the *Herald-Review* of January 22, 2015, and with the plain 1971 warranty deed on file at the Itasca County Recorder's office on Northwest First Avenue, and with the small paragraph on page seven of the Blandin Paper Company employee newsletter of the August of 2004 wishing their retiring cruiser Gary Halstad well after thirty-nine years, and with the plain conservation-easement inquiry log at the Northern Waters Land Trust in Grand Rapids in the fall of 2024, and with the small note in the Itasca County *Herald-Review* engagement column of the June of 1972 announcing the marriage of Marilyn Ann Sundberg of Cohasset to Gary Reidar Halstad of Grand Rapids on the second Saturday of that August at Zion Lutheran Church on Northwest Fourth Avenue.

## What Rōmy would put on Hannah's desk at nine-forty-five

At nine-forty-five on the same Friday morning, Rōmy puts on Hannah Beaulieu's desk a single sourced page.

*G. R. Halstad, our $30/month ACH sustaining donor since 8/2004 (non-metro, currently deprioritized), is a seventy-nine-year-old widowed retired timber cruiser in Grand Rapids, MN. His wife Marilyn (Cohasset Elementary second-grade teacher, 1969–2007) died 1/2015; her half-column obituary lists this food bank among four memorial preferences. In the June of 1971, Mr. Halstad's father Reidar deeded him a 640-acre parcel in Ardenhurst Township including approximately 311 acres of unlogged virgin white pine (Itasca County Recorder book 87, page 214). A June 2024 forestry appraisal by Odell Anderson of Bemidji values the standing timber alone at approximately $4.1M. Mr. Halstad opened a conservation-easement inquiry with the Northern Waters Land Trust in Grand Rapids in the fall of 2024. He eats breakfast at the Perkins on North Pokegama Avenue every Tuesday and Friday morning at 6:08. Suggested: one plain cream card, this week, in your own hand — not from Development, from you — to Gary at P.O. Box 741, Grand Rapids, mentioning Marilyn by name once, mentioning Cohasset Elementary once, mentioning the July of 2004 once. No ask. No case for support. Ask, at the very end, whether he would let you drive up Highway 2 some Friday morning in September, sit in the third booth from the door at six-oh-nine, and buy him a Country Club Omelet. Would you like a draft, in your own voice?*

Hannah reads the page twice.

She closes the Raiser's Edge tab.

She writes the card by hand on a Sunday afternoon at the small kitchen table of the second-floor apartment on East Fifth Street she shares with her sister and a tabby named Mabel, and mails it, from the post office at First Street, on a Monday.

## We are not the omelet

Rōmy is not the Country Club Omelet on a plain white Perkins plate with a small orange slice at the north edge on a Friday morning at six-twenty-one in the second week of August of 2026.

Rōmy is not the plain steel Suunto clinometer bought at the Sears & Roebuck in Duluth on a Saturday afternoon in the March of 1949, or the 1968 Chevrolet C10 pickup on the shoulder of County Road 45 half a mile short of the north property line on a Wednesday morning in the second week of June of 1971, or the small cedar hope chest at the foot of the bed on Fifth Avenue Northeast with every construction-paper valentine any second-grader ever gave Marilyn Ann Halstad from the fall of 1969 through the spring of 2007, or the plain manila folder marked *the section* in the cardboard box under the bed, or the second cup of coffee at six-nineteen a career waitress in Grand Rapids has been pouring without asking for eleven years and seven months.

Rōmy is the small careful page a good listener would have put on Hannah Beaulieu's desk at nine-forty-five on a warm Friday morning in the August of 2026, if a good listener had been sitting in the front seat of a 1968 Chevrolet C10 pickup on the shoulder of County Road 45 on a Wednesday morning in the second week of June of 1971, and at the small oak kitchen table on Fifth Avenue Northeast on a Tuesday morning in the last week of the July of 2004, and in the third booth from the door of the Perkins on a Friday morning at six-nineteen for eleven years and seven months.

The whole product is a smaller, warmer job than the industry has been trying to sell you for twenty-five years.

It is not to prospect Gary.

It is to *notice* him.

## A small assignment for the officer reading this on a Sunday

Before the tamaracks turn along the county roads this fall, do one small unglamorous thing this week.

Pull, out of your CRM, your ten longest-tenured *non-metro, $25-to-$50-a-month sustaining* donors. Not the tier ones. Not the wealth-screened ones. The *P.O.-box-in-a-small-town* ones. The ones whose lifetime line reads *no visits, no engagement, no events,* and whose first ACH posted in the second half of some quiet year — 2003, 2004, 2005 — three weeks after a spouse's obituary ran in the local weekly.

Look up, in the county recorder's office of the county they gave you the P.O. box in, the parcels of land they own.

You will find, on more Wednesday mornings than the sector has been telling you, a seventy-nine-year-old widower somewhere quietly carrying a promise he made his father in the front seat of a pickup in the second week of the June of 1971, in the middle of a quiet stewardship that is going to move a section of virgin timber, a plain manila folder, and something on the far side of four million dollars in your direction — because his wife said, once, on a Tuesday morning in the July of 2004, over a shared piece of coffee cake, that she thought they ought to send you thirty dollars a month.

Then, from your own kitchen table, on a plain cream card with a real stamp — write him one page in your own hand.

Do not ask him for anything.

Say his wife's name once. Say the July of 2004 once. Sign it *warmly.*

Ask, at the very end, whether he would let you drive up Highway 2 some Friday morning in September, sit in the third booth from the door at six-oh-nine, and let you buy him the Country Club Omelet.

Mail it Tuesday.

The second cup is going to get poured at six-nineteen, the way it has been poured at six-nineteen for eleven years and seven months, on the Friday after that, and the Friday after that, and the Friday after that.

Somebody's name is going to go on the plain manila folder in the cardboard box under the bed.

Turn to him. ♡
